<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lady Six Sky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 741</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Lady Wak Chanil Ajaw</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lady Six Sky was a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> who arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a> in 682 CE and restored its royal dynasty after defeats by Caracol. She governed for her young son K'ak' Tiliw Chan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaak</a> and was shown on monuments in the guise of a warrior ruler. Her rule revived Naranjo as a regional power in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-six-sky.jpg" alt="Lady Six Sky" width="320" height="695" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MA_D367_Maya_stela_24,_Naranjo,_Guatemala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lady Six Sky was a daughter of B'alaj Chan K'awiil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> and of a woman who may have been named Lady B'uluka'l, who carried the prestigious title ochk'in kalo'mte on Stela 24. The reading of the queen's own name is contested, and scholars use the English translation of its readable part, Wak Chan. She came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, at her father's behest, in an arranged marriage meant to bring the city into the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> and Dos Pilas, perhaps in response to Tikal's recent defeat of Calakmul. Her husband may have been a low-status cousin of the previous king, K'ahk' Xiiw Chan Chaahk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Three days after her arrival she performed a burning ritual, recorded on Stela 29 for 31 August 682. Her son K'ak' Tiliw Chan Chaak was born on 6 January 688 and became king at five, with his mother as regent; Stela 46, discovered in 2017, confirmed that he was her son. She never took the title Holy Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a> and kept using the emblem glyph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a>, and Stela 24 counts her son as the 38th ruler rather than the 39th, which suggests she was never the formal head of government. Christophe Helmke notes that her monuments centred on herself well into her son's adulthood.</p><p>Stela 24, of 17 April 699, shows her standing on a captive from the small polity of K'inichil Kab, an unusual representation for a woman, and dressed in the net skirt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> god. She was involved in the sacking of at least nine other polities and in a victorious war against Komkom in April 726. She marked the rare Tikal Cycle period endings of 1 March 695 and 17 November 714 and supervised the production of high-quality pottery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After K'ak' Tiliw Chan Chaak died in his early forties, she seems to have promoted a new ruler, Yax Mayuy Chan Chaak, who may have been his younger brother. A name that is probably hers appears in a scattering ritual at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> on 10 or 11 February 741, which may mark her death, though this is contested. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatiana-proskouriakoff">Tatiana Proskouriakoff</a> first recognised her name in the 1960s and called her the Lady of Tikal because of her emblem glyph, before scholars realised that more than one city used it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lady Six Sky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings traced their descent from founding ancestors, were enthroned in elaborate ceremonies and led in war and ritual, and royal women could hold power, as <b>Lady Six Sky</b> did at Naranjo in the late 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal marriages moved women between dynasties and could refound a kingdom, as when <b>Lady Six Sky</b> came from Dos Pilas to Naranjo in 682.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal marriage linked the dynasty to Naranjo through <b>Lady Six Sky</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a></li><li><blockquote>In 682 <b>Lady Six Sky</b> (Ix Wak Jalam Chan Ajaw), daughter of B'ajlaj Chan K'awiil of Dos Pilas, arrived to restore the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/naranjo">Naranjo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.45,-90.3&amp;year=731" aria-label="Dos Pilas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lady Six Sky"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 6148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 6148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.45,-90.3&amp;year=731">What was here in 731 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
